From: The LineageOS Project
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Follow the telephony and icu APIs that moved

The second round only diffed the app's android.* references against the boot
classpath. Everything it reaches under other prefixes went unchecked, and
that is where the rest of the breaks were:

  libcore.icu.ICU.getTZDataVersion()           gone; android.icu has it
  libcore.util.ZoneInfoDB                      moved into the i18n apex
  com.android.internal.telephony.Phone         15 methods LG added
  com.android.internal.telephony.PhoneFactory  getCommandsInterface()
  com.android.ims.ImsManager                   isEmergencyCallAvailableOverWfc

The ones with a current equivalent are ported to it: android.icu's
TimeZone.getTZDataVersion(), com.android.i18n.timezone.ZoneInfoDb,
getCommandsInterfaces()[0], and the apnType overload of
getDataConnectionState().

The fifteen Phone methods are LG's own additions to telephony-common -
engineering mode, modem NV items, preferred network type and the band/MIMO
test hooks - and there is nothing in AOSP behind them, so the calls are
dropped: the screens open and sit at their defaults instead of dying in
onCreate. getEmodeInfoPage is the only one with a return value, and yields "".
isEmergencyCallAvailableOverWfc is likewise LG's; false is what the caller
already handles.

The rest came from watching the app rather than from diffing.

com.lge.hiddenmenu.hal.*HalInterface.connectToProxy catches RemoteException
around its getService(), but a HIDL lookup that finds nothing throws
NoSuchElementException, which goes straight through and takes the singleton's
class initialiser with it - so the screen dies in onCreate and every later
attempt gets ExceptionInInitializerError. LG wrote the NoSuchElementException
catch in six of these sixteen classes; this adds the same catch to the ten
that were missing it, so a HAL that is absent or failed to start leaves the
screen empty rather than gone.

The menus hand off to nineteen LG and mediatek apps that are not part of this
build - the GNSS test tools, the DM/FOTA clients, the operator hidden menu,
the mtk logger - and the top menu's preference intents aim at more of the same
(the UICC tool inside their Stk). Overriding startActivity on the sixteen
activities that launch them catches every one: the entries stay listed and do
nothing. The list came from pairing the const-strings that feed
Intent.setClassName with what "pm path" answers for on the device. Those
intents live in compiled preference xml, which cannot be edited without
decoding the app's resources and renumbering the ids its smali has baked in.

TetheringMode reads and writes the USB function mask, which needs MANAGE_USB.
The app does not request it and cannot be given it for the same reason, so it
reports "no function" and the setter is dropped.

DebugScreen is declared to run in com.android.phone's process, which is uid
radio - not the uid com.lge.hiddenmenu holds LGHIDDEN under - so binding its
own package's banner service is a permission denial thrown out of onResume.
The banner is an overlay over the screen; the screen works without it.

DataDebugScreen reads the same com.lge integer twice, and the first patch in
this series only answered the first of the two. The value is never used after
it is computed, and the app's own else branch is to take zero, so the second
lookup is answered by taking that branch.

GraphView divides by what charge_full_design reads back, which is zero when
it cannot be read at all.

TouchTest asks framework-res for config_showNavigationBar, which is gone -
whether there is a navigation bar is the policy's answer now, not a build
resource. joan has one, and that is the branch the screen wants: it drops the
capacitive key test and keeps the on-screen one.
